WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:12.367
Episode three: branches. A branch is a separate line of work: you try an idea there, and main does not move until you decide.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:33.990
git branch lists them: only main, with a star. switch -c creates a branch and moves onto it; now HEAD names weekend.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:51.397
A commit on weekend. The graph shows the two pointers: weekend one commit ahead, main where it was.

00:00:51.497 --> 00:01:11.125
Back on main, and merge. Main had not moved, so Git just moves it ahead to weekend: a fast-forward, no new commit.

00:01:11.225 --> 00:01:45.175
Now two lines that both move. A party branch adds a line to the list; meanwhile main gets a new file, drinks. The graph forks.

00:01:45.275 --> 00:02:02.031
This merge cannot be a fast-forward: Git writes a merge commit, a commit with two parents, and the graph closes again.

00:02:02.131 --> 00:02:34.186
And a conflict. The menu says pizza. A veggie branch changes the same line to salad; main changes it to pasta.

00:02:34.286 --> 00:03:01.293
The merge stops: CONFLICT in menu.txt. Status lists it under unmerged paths. In the file, Git wrote both versions: ours between the less-than signs and the equals, theirs down to the greater-than signs.

00:03:01.393 --> 00:03:19.023
Resolving is writing the content you want, without the markers. add marks it resolved, and commit closes the merge, with the message Git prepared.

00:03:19.123 --> 00:03:43.405
The graph tells the whole story. The merged branches can go: branch -d refuses one that is not merged yet.

00:03:43.505 --> 00:03:52.566 line:6%
Next episode: stash, and a remote to share the work with.
